WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:14.020
CAMPBELL: I'm Ken Campbell.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.550
I'm the director
of the News Office.

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:19.484
And today's session has become
an embargoed private briefing

00:00:19.484 --> 00:00:21.150
for the night Science
Journalism Fellows

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:26.100
at MIT the embargo is for
11 AM Eastern Daylight

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.130
Time on Tuesday, May 2, which
means that it should not

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.450
appear in any publication-- any
place in the world before then.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:37.350
And we've been getting great
cooperation from the press

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.020
to adhere to this embargo.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.750
We've used this embargo because
this is a very complex story,

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.050
and it's not one
that you can just

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.787
whip off in a half an hour.

00:00:47.787 --> 00:00:49.620
So you need to have
some time to look at it,

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.130
and study it, and
then write a more

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.320
comprehensive story
than is ordinarily

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.090
available under deadline.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.640
So I'd like to turn
the proceedings over

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.210
to Michael Dertouzos, the
Chairman of the Commission,

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.430
and first, Paul Gray,
the President of MIT,

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.324
will speak and introduce
Professor Dertouzos.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.440
GRAY: Good morning, and
thank you for coming.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.720
We're here today because
we have an urgent message

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.540
to convey to America
to its financial

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.930
and industrial community, and
to the millions of Americans who

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.870
rely on industry for
their livelihoods.

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:31.030
The message is that
American productivity

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.940
is slipping, even though the
United States is still the most

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.652
productive nation in the world.

00:01:36.652 --> 00:01:38.110
The weaknesses in
American industry

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:42.460
concern the way people think,
cooperate, invest, manage

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.230
and organize
themselves, as well as

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:47.260
the ways they become
educated, use technology,

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:51.010
learn a new job, and
interact with government.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.080
We need to rethink the
way we do business,

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.360
and the way we
live in the world.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:56.920
International
industry is driving

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.840
along a highway of many lanes.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:02.890
The US is ahead right now, but
we're driving in the slow lane.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.410
Other nations are
speeding along behind us,

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:08.080
and some are about to zoom past.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.330
Why is MIT concerned?

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.190
As a science-based
university concerned

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.410
with the education of
engineers and scientists,

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:17.530
MIT is deeply involved
in industry, and science,

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.810
technology, and productivity.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.300
We believe manufacturing
is absolutely

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.450
essential to this
nation's economic future.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.850
Technology is the main engine
that drives the economy.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.660
That's the message MIT
economist Robert Solow

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.570
has been teaching for 30 years.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:36.520
The problem of US
productivity has

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.730
been studied by economists,
by social scientists,

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:42.460
by engineers, and by scientists.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.890
But up to now, no one has
thrown them all together

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.320
in a room to thrash
out this problem,

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:48.910
to study it from
the factory floor

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:52.240
on up in the United States
and Europe and in Japan,

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.630
and to see what MIT and other
universities might do about it.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:59.634
That's why I appointed 16
MIT faculty, from the Schools

00:02:59.634 --> 00:03:02.050
of Science, Engineering, from
the Department of Economics,

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:06.820
and from Social Sciences to
this committee in November 1986,

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:09.370
and asked them to
wrestle with this problem

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:12.310
until they came to a shared
perspective, a common view,

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.170
about the problem and about
what might be done about it.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:17.440
I should note that this is
the first such effort we've

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.440
undertaken at MIT, at
least since World War II,

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:24.470
and perhaps going back
much earlier than that.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:27.070
We have with us today professors
Michael Dertouzos, Richard

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:30.310
Lester, Robert Solow, Suzanne
Berger, and Lester Thurow,

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.847
six of the 16 commissioners.

00:03:32.847 --> 00:03:34.930
Michael Dertouzos, the
Chairman of the Commission,

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.840
is Professor of Computer Science
and Electrical Engineering,

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.850
and Director of
the MIT Laboratory

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:41.470
for Computer Science.

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:43.150
He's played a major
role in fostering

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:45.460
cooperation between
industry, and universities

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.500
in the computer
field, and he's been

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.690
a leader in innovative
curriculum development

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:51.790
and computer science.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:54.280
Made in America-- Regaining
the Productive Edge

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.130
reports on the conclusions
of this commission.

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:58.930
What MIT and other
leading universities

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:02.530
should do to contribute to
improving US productivity,

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:06.677
and what industry must do to
get us back in the fast lane.

00:04:06.677 --> 00:04:08.260
Now, Professor
Dertouzos will give you

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:10.810
a 20-minute summary of
two years of work, which

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:12.850
involved more than 75 people.

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:15.430
He will then introduce the
other panelists who are here,

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:17.500
and we will try to
respond to your questions.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:18.000
Michael.

00:04:28.625 --> 00:04:29.750
DERTOUZOS: Thank you, Paul.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:36.990
Good morning, ladies
and gentlemen.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.630
The message that
we are in trouble

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:42.270
is by no means shared today--

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.850
nor was it shared when
we started our work

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:47.370
--by the people of this nation.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.280
Some people said then,
as they say today,

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.240
that the US is in big trouble.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:55.800
They say we've lost automobiles,
we've lost textiles,

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:59.760
they say we've lost consumer
electronics, and steel.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.680
They also say that
US products are

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.950
becoming expensive and
of lower quality compared

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:06.810
to foreign products.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:10.980
Our annual trade deficit
is huge, at $120 billion,

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:17.160
and United States productivity
is growing slowly.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.580
But there are others,
especially our leaders,

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.100
who tell us that
we're second to none.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.790
And it is true that, while US
productivity is growing slowly,

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:30.250
it is nevertheless the
highest in the world.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:34.630
Also, unemployment is low, and
our plants are working at close

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.470
to full capacity.

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:37.990
So who is right?

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:41.680
Are we in trouble, or are we OK?

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:43.960
As the President
told you, the verdict

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.450
of this commission
after two years of work

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:49.540
is that the United States'
industrial performance

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:52.240
is indeed in serious trouble.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.770
And this matters a great
deal, because if we do not

00:05:56.770 --> 00:06:02.500
mend our ways, all Americans
will pay the price in terms

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.920
of our standard of living.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:09.910
But to correct this problem,
we must first understand it.

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:12.670
Unfortunately, if
you string together

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:16.870
all the accounts in the press
and all the scholarly studies

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.930
that report on this issue
and on the weaknesses

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:22.690
of American industrial
performance,

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:29.240
you get a list so large
that it is unbelievable.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:34.430
So we set out to separate the
important from the unimportant

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:36.210
in our own way.

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:39.050
We took a different approach
from most other studies

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:43.010
that treat the US economy
like a box with knobs

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:46.580
that you turn to control
exchange rates or interest

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:47.750
rates.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:51.560
It's not that we do not believe
in macroeconomic factors,

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:56.300
we just don't think they are
adequate to explain or correct

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:58.020
the nation's
productivity problems.

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:06.520
So we based our work
on a bottom-up study

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.520
of these eight industries,
which are shown here, along

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.850
with the trade balance.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:16.690
They include winners and
losers, and together they

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:21.520
account for some 28% of
United States' manufacturing.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:24.070
We split ourselves
into eight groups,

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:28.390
and carried out 550 interviews
in three continents,

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:32.110
concentrating on what
happens at the factory floor,

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:35.780
in the office, and
in the board room.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:39.440
We also formed a ninth group,
which looked at education,

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:42.860
both at school and at work.

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:45.950
We then took all these
results-- from the eight studies

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:49.280
of the industries and the
ninth group of education

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:51.800
--and we pulled them
together and searched

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:56.000
for common patterns
of weaknesses.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:59.120
None of the weaknesses
that emerged

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:02.990
from this bottom-up
study will be new to you,

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:06.800
since, as I said earlier,
every conceivable weakness

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:09.600
has been brought forth.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:13.220
What is new in
the MIT results is

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:17.160
which of the many
reported weaknesses

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:22.860
emerged as actual patterns
from this bottom-up study.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:24.180
There are only six.

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:34.830
First weakness--
outdated strategies.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.090
In the best US and
foreign companies,

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:40.240
mass production has
given way to short runs

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:44.440
of sophisticated, high-quality,
customized products.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:47.860
Yet we have found that
the bulk of US companies

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:51.400
still cling to an
inflexible mass production

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:55.540
strategy that worked well
in the days of Henry Ford.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:59.530
Workers are still
treated like cogs

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:03.820
in a wheel that must be kept
turning no matter what--

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:06.550
the wheel of production.

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:08.940
This older, rigid
system turns out

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:13.060
to be less productive than
the newer, more flexible ones.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:15.120
So that is one of our
outdated strategies,

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.290
clinging to mass production.

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:19.410
But there is a second.

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:22.090
We are also parochial.

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:24.220
We still look to
the United States

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:26.530
as the sole source
of everything,

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:29.340
and particularly of technology.

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:32.530
US companies, for example,
were late in introducing

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:36.760
the basic oxygen furnace or
the Kawabata measuring system

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:37.990
in textiles.

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:41.310
Both were foreign technologies.

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:44.040
We also assume
that others share--

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:47.280
or at least they should
share --our tastes

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:51.750
in the way we do business,
as did one of our companies

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:55.820
that we studied that shipped
a huge quantity of sweatshirts

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.070
to Europe, expecting
that they would sell

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:01.430
as well as they were selling at
home, and found out, of course,

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:03.650
that they did not.

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:08.360
But let us move on to
the second weakness--

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:11.330
short time horizons.

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:13.490
Now here is a familiar pattern.

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:16.370
A famous American
electronics company

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:19.850
faces competition at the
low end of its market--

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:23.990
say, the transistor radios,
--from a Japanese company,

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:25.690
or companies.

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:29.720
It abandons that
market to Japan.

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:33.080
It holds on to its higher-end
commercial electronics

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:35.990
products, and then
it turns around

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:43.180
and it buys a rental car
company or a finance company.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:45.230
Now, they do this
because they believe

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:50.100
that it shows a higher return
on investment, a higher ROI.

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:53.660
Meanwhile, the Japanese
company builds its knowledge

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:56.090
and expertise in
this area, builds up

00:10:56.090 --> 00:11:00.290
market share, holding
profits low or zero,

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:03.710
and eventually assaults
the higher-end commercial

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:06.300
electronic products of
the American company.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:10.340
And so it is that in consumer
electronics, where we once

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:14.810
produced 90% of what we
consumed, we're now down to 4%.

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:20.330
By not sticking
to their knitting,

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:23.450
as we call this
behavior, US companies

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:29.980
end up losing both
expertise and market share.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:33.320
Now, it is often said that
the high cost of capital

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:35.900
is the culprit that
pushes our companies

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:40.180
toward a short-term orientation.

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:42.450
The commission agrees
that this is a factor,

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:44.850
but we have found
other factors as well.

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:48.800
For example, these ROI
calculations, when in error,

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:53.120
tend to favor the short-term,
and the investing strategies

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:55.700
of fund managers
who control a lot

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:57.779
of the stock of
these companies value

00:11:57.779 --> 00:11:59.195
companies with
short-term profits.

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:14.390
Third weakness--
technological weaknesses

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:16.310
in development and production.

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:19.460
The United States is
first in the world

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:21.350
in inventing new products.

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:27.110
Transistors, color television,
VCRs were all invented here,

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:31.220
yet none of these products, or
hardly any of these products,

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:34.040
are made here today.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:37.250
And the products that we
make, for example automobiles,

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:39.080
have a larger
number of defects--

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:41.360
in the case of cars,
almost two to one

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:43.850
--compared to Japanese products.

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:47.840
It's almost as if we've spent
so much energy inventing

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:50.300
that there is none
left to convert

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:53.600
our inventions into products.

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:58.460
In fact, we do spend 2/3
of our industrial R&amp;D

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:03.410
money on the inventing side,
and 1/3 on the processes

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:06.170
that make products out
of these interventions.

00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:08.770
In Japan, this
ratio is a reverse.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:14.570
Beyond that, our
attitudes, too, favor

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:17.000
intervention over production.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:20.080
We glorify the
inventor, and we feel

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:25.000
that a career in manufacturing
spells sheer drudgery.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:30.020
We tend to design
quickly, and debug later,

00:13:30.020 --> 00:13:34.510
whereas in the best companies,
both here and abroad,

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:38.870
people build quality
into the design.

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:41.270
We also look for
breakthrough strategies.

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:45.830
We look for that aha that is
going to solve our problems,

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:49.940
and we do not believe so much
in the incremental, steady,

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:53.990
gradual, improvements that are
favored by the world's best

00:13:53.990 --> 00:13:55.340
companies.

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:59.450
We even have this word, or
this phrase, in our culture.

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:02.510
We say, if it ain't
broke yet, don't fix it.

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:11.280
Fourth weakness-- neglect
of human resources.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:14.310
First and foremost, we neglect
education at the kindergarten

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:17.010
through 12th grade level.

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:21.290
The United States, a nation
that pioneered mass education,

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:22.850
is now ranking--

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:25.460
through its high
school students,

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:29.360
worldwide, in various exams
from math and geography

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:33.890
and reading --between
8th and 15th

00:14:33.890 --> 00:14:36.930
among the nations of the world.

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:42.330
And this slippage is happening
at a very wrong time, when

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:44.400
the new systems
of production call

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:48.990
for greater technical
literacy in the workforce.

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:52.590
This educational problem
of the United States youth

00:14:52.590 --> 00:14:56.870
is perhaps the biggest
weakness that we face.

00:14:56.870 --> 00:14:58.010
But there is more.

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:00.920
We also neglect our workforce.

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:03.830
We tend to view labor
as a cost factor

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:07.250
to be minimized rather
than a precious asset

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:09.890
to be nurtured and developed.

00:15:09.890 --> 00:15:11.960
Let me explain.

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:17.380
Our vocational schools
are not effective,

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:20.500
and on-the-job training, on
which our companies spend

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:23.500
over $50 or $60 billion
a year, involves

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:27.100
either remedial training,
like reading and arithmetic,

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:32.000
or following Joe around to
learn how things are done.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:36.170
By contrast, in
Japan and in Germany,

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:40.520
workers undergo
systematic job rotations

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:44.390
that makes them broader, more
flexible, and more willing

00:15:44.390 --> 00:15:47.020
to learn new skills.

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:51.890
As a result, when a plant
fails in the United States,

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:54.890
some centralized team
has to be brought over

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:59.690
to correct the damage, and that
takes time and induces delays.

00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:04.120
In Japan and Germany, most of
the time, failures of plants

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:06.550
are handled by the
operators, because they've

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:09.070
been trained broadly
enough to understand

00:16:09.070 --> 00:16:13.120
all the aspects of their
plant, thereby saving time

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:17.520
and money for their companies.

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:21.860
Fifth weakness--
failures of cooperation.

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:23.480
We found this at several levels.

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:25.850
First, there are
failures of cooperation

00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:28.220
within our companies.

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:29.720
Here is a pattern.

00:16:29.720 --> 00:16:33.590
The designers design a product,
they toss it over the wall

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:35.020
to the production people.

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:37.520
The production people
try to build it.

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:39.080
It doesn't work very well.

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:41.210
They write some
corrections, toss it back

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:43.270
over the wall for redesign.

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:46.730
This ping pong goes
on and on, and can

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:52.280
be traced to induce delays
in the production process.

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:55.360
In copiers, in automobiles,
and in other products,

00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:58.420
we have found these delays
to amount as much as two

00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:01.000
to one compared
to companies that

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:04.069
do not follow these practices.

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:06.250
But there are other
failures of cooperation.

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:08.740
There are failures of
cooperation between the company

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:10.720
and its suppliers.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:12.950
In the US automobile
business, for example,

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:16.900
as in other businesses, we
find in this country thousands

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:18.060
of suppliers.

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:20.319
Whereas, in the
best of companies,

00:17:20.319 --> 00:17:24.339
both here and abroad, one
finds hundreds of suppliers--

00:17:24.339 --> 00:17:26.447
same business.

00:17:26.447 --> 00:17:28.030
Also, there is a
difference in the way

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:29.890
we deal with our suppliers.

00:17:29.890 --> 00:17:33.490
In the US, the
relationship is based

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:37.970
on an adversarial thing, the
contract, and the spectre

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:40.550
of potential litigation.

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:43.940
Whereas in the best
companies, the relationship

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:48.680
is one of cooperation and
sharing a mutual goal.

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:53.290
We also found failures
of cooperation

00:17:53.290 --> 00:17:54.820
among competing companies.

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:57.730
Now, you would say, why should
competing companies cooperate?

00:17:57.730 --> 00:18:01.960
For one thing, to
develop common standards.

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:04.270
In the numerically-controlled
machine tool business,

00:18:04.270 --> 00:18:08.080
for example, we have
found that in this country

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:10.960
we had great
difficulty in agreeing

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:15.640
on the language for these
machines to talk to each other.

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:17.630
The Japanese have
no such problem.

00:18:17.630 --> 00:18:20.470
They got one company to
construct the controllers,

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:21.967
and they settled
on the language.

00:18:21.967 --> 00:18:23.800
Never mind that it
wasn't the best language.

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:25.444
It was common.

00:18:25.444 --> 00:18:26.860
And apparently,
we haven't learned

00:18:26.860 --> 00:18:30.310
yet, because in the area
of computer networks,

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:32.710
we have exactly the
same problem today.

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:36.617
We have one standard for almost
every company that we have

00:18:36.617 --> 00:18:37.450
that makes networks.

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:45.880
Sixth weakness-- government
and industry at cross purposes.

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:50.170
When we started our study,
our friends from business

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:52.930
told us that too
much government was

00:18:52.930 --> 00:18:56.320
what was causing their
difficulties, and America's

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:58.360
productivity plight.

00:18:58.360 --> 00:19:02.380
Now our industry studies
did not bear this out,

00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:04.780
nor did they bear
out the opposite.

00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:08.740
What we did find is that
government and industry

00:19:08.740 --> 00:19:12.250
are going their own way,
following their own agendas,

00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:15.970
oblivious to each other's
needs and aspirations.

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:22.990
Now, if you're becoming
depressed by all this gloom,

00:19:22.990 --> 00:19:25.480
let me say that, in
our industry studies,

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:30.280
we were also heartened to
find common patterns of best

00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:34.970
practices, those
companies that do

00:19:34.970 --> 00:19:38.270
as well or better than
their foreign adversaries

00:19:38.270 --> 00:19:41.110
in the international arena.

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:43.180
Since what we found
out is included

00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:46.510
in our recommendations
of best practices,

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:49.210
or imperatives if
you wish, I am going

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:53.120
to try to save time and move on.

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:57.030
Now, studying the
weaknesses in America,

00:19:57.030 --> 00:19:59.660
as well as the
strengths, isn't enough,

00:19:59.660 --> 00:20:02.330
because that takes
us only up to now.

00:20:02.330 --> 00:20:04.760
We need to look at the future.

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:09.110
So the commission tried to
identify future trends that

00:20:09.110 --> 00:20:11.420
would govern business.

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:14.875
And we came up with
three broad such trends.

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:22.750
We believe that the world will
become even more international

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:27.350
than it is today, and
therefore even smaller.

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:32.370
Second, people will expect
and will get higher-quality,

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:35.450
more sophisticated products.

00:20:35.450 --> 00:20:38.390
Third, technology,
and particularly

00:20:38.390 --> 00:20:41.270
information technology,
biotechnology,

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:43.580
and material
technology, will become

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:47.240
more critical to the
production of goods.

00:20:47.240 --> 00:20:51.390
Now these trends
spell out a quickening

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:54.930
of the pace, the
need for US companies

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:59.760
to be more agile, more
flexible and more sophisticated

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:04.050
if we are to compete
effectively in the world.

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:06.420
Now, the MIT commission
combined what

00:21:06.420 --> 00:21:10.140
we learned from studying these
weaknesses, the strengths,

00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:11.700
and the future trends.

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:16.940
We put them all together to come
up with our recommendations.

00:21:16.940 --> 00:21:21.340
There are five imperatives
that, if followed by industry,

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:23.040
by government, and
by the nation's

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:25.740
educational institutions,
will help America

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:28.500
regain its productive edge.

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:31.680
I will describe them
briefly and broadly.

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:33.870
The book spells
out what they mean

00:21:33.870 --> 00:21:38.260
for each of the players,
namely industry, government,

00:21:38.260 --> 00:21:42.040
and educational institutions.

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:48.520
First imperative-- focus on
producing well the new way.

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:50.470
What do we mean by that?

00:21:50.470 --> 00:21:54.370
For one thing, we mean
that we must stop putting

00:21:54.370 --> 00:21:57.920
finance ahead of production.

00:21:57.920 --> 00:22:00.610
This business of manipulating
financial instruments

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:07.600
from one mirror to another is
no way to create new wealth.

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:11.280
And we must focus as much
attention on production

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:18.010
processes as we do today on
the inventing of new products.

00:22:18.010 --> 00:22:21.190
Our managers, too,
will no longer

00:22:21.190 --> 00:22:26.140
be able to remain detached,
saying, as they often do today,

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:27.310
I can do anything.

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:28.300
Point me.

00:22:28.300 --> 00:22:31.290
What do you want, paper
clips, or jet aircraft?

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:33.430
I can make it.

00:22:33.430 --> 00:22:35.660
Tomorrow's best
managers will need

00:22:35.660 --> 00:22:38.463
to know their
product intimately.

00:22:42.410 --> 00:22:47.800
Second imperative-- cultivate
a new economic citizenship.

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:50.820
As we have seen, the
old rigid system,

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:54.800
where each individual
worker and each supplier

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:58.720
is a cog in this large
wheel of production,

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:00.800
and if they don't
behave, you toss them

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:02.750
so that the wheel
can keep turning,

00:23:02.750 --> 00:23:07.410
that old system of
production works no more.

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:11.760
Employees must now be given
greater breadth, greater

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:16.100
responsibility, and
broader on-the-job training

00:23:16.100 --> 00:23:20.730
in order to be productive within
new sophisticated and flexible

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:24.160
systems of production.

00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:27.700
And since so much more is
being asked of the workforce,

00:23:27.700 --> 00:23:31.330
they must also be rewarded,
both with participation

00:23:31.330 --> 00:23:36.210
in the firm's gains, and with
greater employment stability.

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:39.440
But we can go beyond
the utilitarian notions

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:43.520
that we need to treat workers
well so that they will produce

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:44.820
better, which is true.

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:46.520
We can go beyond that.

00:23:46.520 --> 00:23:50.510
There is an unprecedented
opportunity here

00:23:50.510 --> 00:23:53.090
to give the United
States workforce

00:23:53.090 --> 00:23:58.600
a new sense of mastery of their
work environment and greater

00:23:58.600 --> 00:24:00.310
job satisfaction.

00:24:00.310 --> 00:24:03.947
And that's why we call it
a new economic citizenship.

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:11.180
Third imperative-- blend
cooperation and individualism.

00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:13.940
When the stakes
are high, we have

00:24:13.940 --> 00:24:18.950
shown that we can do both,
as do the Boston Celtics,

00:24:18.950 --> 00:24:25.190
or, more seriously, the best of
companies in the United States.

00:24:25.190 --> 00:24:28.190
Now, we can take some
practical steps, too.

00:24:28.190 --> 00:24:31.460
We can begin doing
this by flattening

00:24:31.460 --> 00:24:33.650
organizational
hierarchies, which

00:24:33.650 --> 00:24:37.220
now run 17 layers deep,
from the boss to the lowest

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:39.500
person in the company.

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:43.550
And we can begin rewarding,
with bonuses, both individualism

00:24:43.550 --> 00:24:48.140
and cooperation in companies.

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:51.860
Fourth imperative-- learn to
live in the world economy.

00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:56.870
We must learn foreign languages,
cultures, and practices

00:24:56.870 --> 00:24:59.810
if we are to compete
in the world arena.

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:03.920
And we must shop internationally
for the best technologies,

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:08.000
wherever they may
happen to arise.

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:10.670
We must also establish
national policies

00:25:10.670 --> 00:25:14.030
that will help us save
for productive investment

00:25:14.030 --> 00:25:16.470
and balance our budget.

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:19.160
Finally, we should
invest in the future

00:25:19.160 --> 00:25:24.740
by investing in infrastructure,
both old and new.

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:26.390
Just as an example,
the commission

00:25:26.390 --> 00:25:28.700
thinks that we should
begin investing

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:32.420
in a new national
information network that

00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:34.550
will be to information
and business

00:25:34.550 --> 00:25:37.850
in the 21st century what
the highway system was

00:25:37.850 --> 00:25:40.310
to the transportation
of goods and the economy

00:25:40.310 --> 00:25:42.930
in this century.

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:46.220
Let me now conclude with
what the commission believes

00:25:46.220 --> 00:25:49.280
is an important observation.

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:53.390
These five imperatives
are not a menu.

00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:57.040
They are not a menu from
which you can pick and choose.

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:13.660
They work together-- let's
get them in focus here

00:26:13.660 --> 00:26:17.230
--they work together like
a well-oiled mechanism

00:26:17.230 --> 00:26:21.040
to bring human resources
and technology together

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:25.660
into a highly-productive
overall system.

00:26:25.660 --> 00:26:29.710
In fact, this is the main reason
that when we studied the best

00:26:29.710 --> 00:26:32.860
practices in the US,
we found that they

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:36.370
didn't diffuse as widely
as we thought they should.

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:39.010
I mean, if these companies
are doing so well,

00:26:39.010 --> 00:26:41.890
why aren't their
practices being copied?

00:26:41.890 --> 00:26:45.760
We believe that the reason
is that those companies that

00:26:45.760 --> 00:26:49.280
are doing so well are
doing most, if not all,

00:26:49.280 --> 00:26:50.830
of these things.

00:26:50.830 --> 00:26:53.740
As a result, if another
company wants to imitate them,

00:26:53.740 --> 00:26:56.320
they must undergo
so many changes--

00:26:56.320 --> 00:26:59.825
and so many wrenching
changes --that it's not easy.

00:26:59.825 --> 00:27:03.620
And that's why those
practices are not diffusing.

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:09.190
Now, in closing we of the
MIT Commission on Industrial

00:27:09.190 --> 00:27:13.160
Productivity believe that
industry, government,

00:27:13.160 --> 00:27:15.670
and the educational institutions
of the United States

00:27:15.670 --> 00:27:20.680
can indeed pull together to
carry out these imperatives.

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:23.560
This will require a
great deal of effort

00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:29.720
and a considerable change of
attitude, but it must be done.

00:27:29.720 --> 00:27:34.270
If we want to live well,
we must produce well.

00:27:34.270 --> 00:27:36.370
There are no shortcuts.

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:39.850
On behalf of the
commission, thank you.

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:43.420
Now, as you ask questions,
please identify yourselves

00:27:43.420 --> 00:27:45.220
and your organization.

00:27:45.220 --> 00:27:47.350
And may I ask the panel
to please come up.

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:53.470
We have no floor
microphones, so we will have

00:27:53.470 --> 00:27:55.330
to rephrase your questions.

00:27:57.990 --> 00:28:01.780
And before we start, let
me introduce our panelists.

00:28:01.780 --> 00:28:04.000
You've already met
President Paul Gray.

00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:06.850
Next to him is
Professor Robert Solow,

00:28:06.850 --> 00:28:09.880
from our Department of
Economics, Nobel Prize winner

00:28:09.880 --> 00:28:12.250
in Economics, 1987.

00:28:12.250 --> 00:28:14.160
Next to him, Professor
Richard Lester

00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:16.690
of our Nuclear
Engineering Department.

00:28:16.690 --> 00:28:19.630
Professor Lester was Executive
Director of the commission,

00:28:19.630 --> 00:28:22.390
and Professor Solow
was Vice Chair.

00:28:22.390 --> 00:28:26.650
On your extreme right
is Professor Les Thurow,

00:28:26.650 --> 00:28:30.010
Dean of our Sloan
School of Management.

00:28:30.010 --> 00:28:32.815
And, ladies and gentlemen,
we're ready for your questions.

00:28:38.395 --> 00:28:40.150
Yes, sir.

00:28:40.150 --> 00:29:03.610
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:29:03.610 --> 00:29:06.010
DERTOUZOS: The question
is if we can describe

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:07.710
what would happen to
America, presumably

00:29:07.710 --> 00:29:10.150
the standard of
living of America

00:29:10.150 --> 00:29:14.580
that I said would go down if
these imperatives are not met.

00:29:14.580 --> 00:29:17.394
Who would like to
take this question?

00:29:17.394 --> 00:29:19.174
Gentlemen.

00:29:19.174 --> 00:29:19.840
THUROW: Let me--

00:29:19.840 --> 00:29:20.530
DERTOUZOS: Les.

00:29:20.530 --> 00:29:21.696
THUROW: --take a stab at it.

00:29:21.696 --> 00:29:23.540
I mean, there are two things.

00:29:23.540 --> 00:29:27.130
One is that if your
productivity is growing,

00:29:27.130 --> 00:29:29.130
but at a relatively
slow rate, it

00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:30.700
means that parts
of your population

00:29:30.700 --> 00:29:33.670
are going to have falls in their
absolute standard of living.

00:29:33.670 --> 00:29:35.628
And one of the interesting
things at the moment

00:29:35.628 --> 00:29:37.060
in the United States--

00:29:37.060 --> 00:29:38.500
if you talk about
plugging along,

00:29:38.500 --> 00:29:40.510
you look at what's been
happening in the last 10 years

00:29:40.510 --> 00:29:42.640
--if you take the group
the Department of Labor

00:29:42.640 --> 00:29:46.690
calls non-supervisory production
workers, which are about 2/3

00:29:46.690 --> 00:29:50.140
of the American workforce,
that group's real wages, 8%

00:29:50.140 --> 00:29:53.560
below its peak in
1972, and is currently

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:56.062
falling at a rate of a
little less than 1% of year.

00:29:56.062 --> 00:29:57.520
And that-- and,
you know, so if you

00:29:57.520 --> 00:30:00.250
think of the bottom 2/3
of the American workforce,

00:30:00.250 --> 00:30:03.550
the answer is that you
should expect a slow decline

00:30:03.550 --> 00:30:05.124
in your absolute
standard of living.

00:30:05.124 --> 00:30:07.540
The other problem, of course,
is, if the rest of the world

00:30:07.540 --> 00:30:09.164
is doing better, you
also get a decline

00:30:09.164 --> 00:30:10.841
in your relative
standard of living.

00:30:10.841 --> 00:30:12.340
And I suppose a
good example of that

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:15.580
would be Great Britain, where,
in 1900, they had the world's

00:30:15.580 --> 00:30:17.350
highest per capita income.

00:30:17.350 --> 00:30:19.570
Their income didn't
go down, but now they

00:30:19.570 --> 00:30:22.162
have a standard of living
that's about 2/3 that of what

00:30:22.162 --> 00:30:24.370
would occur in the most
advanced developed countries,

00:30:24.370 --> 00:30:26.650
like Germany, the
United States, or Japan.

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:28.552
And so, they're
two-barreled answers.

00:30:28.552 --> 00:30:30.760
Part of the American population
will suffer a decline

00:30:30.760 --> 00:30:32.540
in their absolute
standard of living,

00:30:32.540 --> 00:30:34.540
and most of the
American population

00:30:34.540 --> 00:30:38.840
would suffer a decline in their
relative standard of living.

00:30:38.840 --> 00:30:40.840
DERTOUZOS: Next question.

00:30:40.840 --> 00:30:41.413
Yes, sir.

00:30:41.413 --> 00:30:43.840
BALL: Gerald Ball,
MIT News office.

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:47.550
Was the commission able to
identify any system, nation,

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:49.310
or government that
might well serve

00:30:49.310 --> 00:30:53.380
as a model for the
picture you're presenting?

00:30:53.380 --> 00:30:55.630
DERTOUZOS: Do we have a
nation or a government

00:30:55.630 --> 00:31:00.414
that could act as a model
for what we are saying here?

00:31:00.414 --> 00:31:02.397
Professor Solow.

00:31:02.397 --> 00:31:03.980
SOLOW: There's a
short answer to that.

00:31:03.980 --> 00:31:06.520
I don't think the
commission in any way

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:08.890
believes that we
ought to try to turn

00:31:08.890 --> 00:31:11.860
the US into Japan,
or into West Germany,

00:31:11.860 --> 00:31:14.530
or anything of the sort.

00:31:14.530 --> 00:31:17.170
We have our own ways of
operating, our own culture,

00:31:17.170 --> 00:31:21.040
our own normal
patterns of behavior.

00:31:21.040 --> 00:31:24.340
We think that some of them
have become un-functional,

00:31:24.340 --> 00:31:26.270
are doing us harm
rather than good.

00:31:26.270 --> 00:31:29.380
And those can be
changed without altering

00:31:29.380 --> 00:31:32.020
the fundamental
character of the society.

00:31:32.020 --> 00:31:34.890
We'll still play
baseball in the spring.

00:31:34.890 --> 00:31:36.790
We'll still play
hockey in the winter.

00:31:36.790 --> 00:31:42.550
And the US will look
very much like the US.

00:31:42.550 --> 00:31:46.690
Inside factories and
between corporations,

00:31:46.690 --> 00:31:49.665
that's where the
differences occur.

00:31:49.665 --> 00:31:50.498
DERTOUZOS: Yes, sir.

00:31:50.498 --> 00:31:53.984
AUDIENCE: Apparently,
there is no past solution?

00:31:53.984 --> 00:31:55.972
Apparently there is
no past solution?

00:31:55.972 --> 00:31:56.972
I am Victor [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:56.972 --> 00:31:58.964
I am a writer here.

00:31:58.964 --> 00:32:02.950
And my question is a plural one.

00:32:02.950 --> 00:32:07.112
I don't think a solution is
going to emerge pretty soon.

00:32:07.112 --> 00:32:09.076
It will take time.

00:32:09.076 --> 00:32:13.505
[INAUDIBLE]

00:32:13.505 --> 00:32:14.380
DERTOUZOS: I'm sorry.

00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:15.090
I couldn't hear the question.

00:32:15.090 --> 00:32:15.882
Could you hear it?

00:32:15.882 --> 00:32:17.590
PANELIST: It had to
do with the schedule.

00:32:17.590 --> 00:32:19.690
DERTOUZOS: Somebody rephrase
it from the table, please.

00:32:19.690 --> 00:32:22.240
THUROW: I think the question
is that these kind of changes

00:32:22.240 --> 00:32:23.620
take time.

00:32:23.620 --> 00:32:25.300
And if you're thinking
about, or are we

00:32:25.300 --> 00:32:27.680
talking about a series of
changes over the next 10 years,

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:29.350
or the next 20 years,
and how much time

00:32:29.350 --> 00:32:32.770
is there to make
these kind of changes?

00:32:32.770 --> 00:32:34.625
As I interpreted the
question, that was it.

00:32:34.625 --> 00:32:35.500
DERTOUZOS: Gentlemen.

00:32:35.500 --> 00:32:37.054
Anyone wants to
take that question?

00:32:37.054 --> 00:32:38.470
SOLOW: Well, I
think the answer is

00:32:38.470 --> 00:32:40.390
that this is-- we
are not talking

00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:44.650
about the sort of change
that happens one Thursday

00:32:44.650 --> 00:32:47.140
at 11 o' clock in the morning.

00:32:47.140 --> 00:32:48.970
Many of the things
that we've described--

00:32:48.970 --> 00:32:51.340
that Mike described,
have already

00:32:51.340 --> 00:32:54.730
started to happen in
American industry,

00:32:54.730 --> 00:33:00.370
and they will, we hope,
accelerate in the next years.

00:33:00.370 --> 00:33:02.550
There's not a cliff
that one falls over.

00:33:02.550 --> 00:33:06.940
There's not a catastrophe
hanging out there on the edge

00:33:06.940 --> 00:33:10.570
that one runs the
danger of incurring.

00:33:10.570 --> 00:33:14.460
Lester pointed out that
the cost of not doing this

00:33:14.460 --> 00:33:18.970
is damage to the standard
of living of our children,

00:33:18.970 --> 00:33:22.870
and damage to the relative
position of the United States

00:33:22.870 --> 00:33:25.280
in the world.

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:33.310
It's not a single catastrophic
event, but a drawn out process.

00:33:33.310 --> 00:33:36.100
We start reversing
that tomorrow,

00:33:36.100 --> 00:33:37.870
or in fact, we have
already started

00:33:37.870 --> 00:33:41.600
reversing that last
year in many industries.

00:33:41.600 --> 00:33:42.790
DERTOUZOS: Paul Gray.

00:33:42.790 --> 00:33:45.550
GRAY: These imperatives have
different time constants

00:33:45.550 --> 00:33:48.790
in terms of change
that might occur.

00:33:48.790 --> 00:33:57.730
In some cases, such as changing
the economic partnership,

00:33:57.730 --> 00:34:00.490
as it was described,
within the company--

00:34:00.490 --> 00:34:02.260
changing the way in
which the workforce is

00:34:02.260 --> 00:34:07.480
perceived, managed, used,
change can begin rather quickly.

00:34:07.480 --> 00:34:11.080
Another aspect of
that related weakness

00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:13.210
which was described
by the commission

00:34:13.210 --> 00:34:17.170
hinges on the quality
of pre-college education

00:34:17.170 --> 00:34:20.080
in the United States.

00:34:20.080 --> 00:34:23.620
The fact that that roughly
20% of adult population

00:34:23.620 --> 00:34:27.460
are functionally illiterate,
the fact that in understanding

00:34:27.460 --> 00:34:30.460
of simple mathematics
and in science,

00:34:30.460 --> 00:34:33.250
we tend to rank towards the
bottom in every comparison

00:34:33.250 --> 00:34:34.900
with other
industrialized nations--

00:34:34.900 --> 00:34:37.719
it's going to take a
while to change that.

00:34:37.719 --> 00:34:40.030
It's going to take some
time to bring about change

00:34:40.030 --> 00:34:43.239
in the quality of pre-college
education across the nation,

00:34:43.239 --> 00:34:46.060
but it seems to me we need to
be in on it rather quickly.

00:34:46.060 --> 00:34:49.570
DERTOUZOS: The book lists
approximately 11 or so,

00:34:49.570 --> 00:34:54.429
I think, ways in which
business could undertake

00:34:54.429 --> 00:34:57.310
these imperatives-- translate
or interpret these imperatives.

00:34:57.310 --> 00:34:59.185
I'm not going to mention
them here, all, just

00:34:59.185 --> 00:35:00.370
mention a couple.

00:35:00.370 --> 00:35:03.490
For example, flattening the
organizational hierarchies.

00:35:03.490 --> 00:35:04.990
That can be done overnight.

00:35:04.990 --> 00:35:07.660
Our large companies can
reorganize themselves,

00:35:07.660 --> 00:35:10.090
and they do, overnight.

00:35:10.090 --> 00:35:12.790
Developing and adopting
new measures of quality.

00:35:12.790 --> 00:35:14.890
For example, instead of
measuring the quality only

00:35:14.890 --> 00:35:17.800
in terms of profits,
one could adopt

00:35:17.800 --> 00:35:22.470
explicitly measuring the
quality of the product,

00:35:22.470 --> 00:35:25.660
and keeping track of it.

00:35:25.660 --> 00:35:29.610
Integrating the functions
of design, production,

00:35:29.610 --> 00:35:32.080
marketing-- this has already
been happening in the best

00:35:32.080 --> 00:35:36.610
US companies, the teams in the
Pro Printer Project at IBM,

00:35:36.610 --> 00:35:38.290
and in the Ford Taurus project.

00:35:38.290 --> 00:35:41.300
So there are many of these
ways of attacking early.

00:35:41.300 --> 00:35:43.037
There are others, as
President Gray said,

00:35:43.037 --> 00:35:44.620
that will take much
longer, especially

00:35:44.620 --> 00:35:46.040
in the area of human resources.

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:47.608
More questions.

00:35:47.608 --> 00:35:55.836
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] You said
that you are paying a decent

00:35:55.836 --> 00:36:00.133
product quality, which is
competitive in the world

00:36:00.133 --> 00:36:04.319
market, it is necessary to buy--

00:36:04.319 --> 00:36:07.217
to look at [INAUDIBLE]
worth buying.

00:36:07.217 --> 00:36:13.898
That good for company that
you import [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:13.898 --> 00:36:18.655
On the other hand, you have
those huge trade deficits.

00:36:18.655 --> 00:36:22.069
How do you [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:22.069 --> 00:36:23.610
DERTOUZOS: The
question is, how do we

00:36:23.610 --> 00:36:26.490
reconcile the recommendation
that we shop for the best

00:36:26.490 --> 00:36:28.350
technology, wherever
it may arise,

00:36:28.350 --> 00:36:31.860
with the huge trade deficit
of the United States.

00:36:31.860 --> 00:36:36.017
And the answer is directed to
me, so I will try to answer it.

00:36:39.150 --> 00:36:42.050
Putting restrictions,
doing protectionism,

00:36:42.050 --> 00:36:46.570
is really no way to solve
the problems before us,

00:36:46.570 --> 00:36:48.960
because it invites
retaliation, and it

00:36:48.960 --> 00:36:50.760
invites all kinds
of complications

00:36:50.760 --> 00:36:52.740
that later haunt everybody.

00:36:52.740 --> 00:36:54.977
And I think the essence
of your question--

00:36:54.977 --> 00:36:56.560
if there's one answer
to your question

00:36:56.560 --> 00:36:57.518
--that's protectionist.

00:36:57.518 --> 00:36:58.950
That's what I would say about.

00:36:58.950 --> 00:37:01.830
So we have no
alternative but to go

00:37:01.830 --> 00:37:04.710
for what we've been preaching
throughout the birth

00:37:04.710 --> 00:37:07.440
of this country, which
is a free market economy,

00:37:07.440 --> 00:37:09.810
and buying the best technologies
wherever they arise.

00:37:09.810 --> 00:37:11.700
This is not where
the problem is.

00:37:11.700 --> 00:37:15.836
The problem in America is in
converting the great inventions

00:37:15.836 --> 00:37:17.460
in which we are number
one in the world

00:37:17.460 --> 00:37:18.990
into high-quality products.

00:37:18.990 --> 00:37:20.880
And we simply must
learn to do that.

00:37:20.880 --> 00:37:24.060
And that is what our
imperatives are directed to.

00:37:24.060 --> 00:37:26.440
Next question.

00:37:26.440 --> 00:37:26.940
Yes.

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:28.812
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE],,
Knight Fellow.

00:37:28.812 --> 00:37:32.350
How can the government inspire
companies and businesses

00:37:32.350 --> 00:37:35.007
to meet-- to follow
these imperatives?

00:37:35.007 --> 00:37:37.090
DERTOUZOS: The question
is, how can the government

00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:39.820
inspire the businesses to
follow these imperatives?

00:37:39.820 --> 00:37:41.270
Who would like to take this?

00:37:41.270 --> 00:37:42.150
THUROW: Let me
take a whack at it,

00:37:42.150 --> 00:37:44.608
because I don't think that's
a governmental responsibility.

00:37:44.608 --> 00:37:46.300
In some sense, that's
the responsibility

00:37:46.300 --> 00:37:47.742
of the press and the media.

00:37:47.742 --> 00:37:49.450
Because one of the
things you're supposed

00:37:49.450 --> 00:37:52.510
to do is bring to the American
public-- or any public

00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:54.760
in the world --some view of
what the rest of the world

00:37:54.760 --> 00:37:55.292
looks like.

00:37:55.292 --> 00:37:57.250
And one of the things
we're talking about here,

00:37:57.250 --> 00:37:59.447
in some sense, is that
the rest of the world

00:37:59.447 --> 00:38:01.780
has copied some of the good
things in the United States,

00:38:01.780 --> 00:38:03.940
like mass public
education, and then they've

00:38:03.940 --> 00:38:05.866
upped the intensity
level, and done it

00:38:05.866 --> 00:38:07.240
better than we
have traditionally

00:38:07.240 --> 00:38:09.614
done it, which means we've
got to up our intensity level,

00:38:09.614 --> 00:38:11.450
and kind of leapfrog once again.

00:38:11.450 --> 00:38:14.620
And so I think it's a real
question of, in some sense,

00:38:14.620 --> 00:38:17.087
are the media telling
the average citizen

00:38:17.087 --> 00:38:19.420
in a way in which the average
citizen can understand it,

00:38:19.420 --> 00:38:21.620
that his or her
world has changed,

00:38:21.620 --> 00:38:24.160
and standard, good-old
American operating procedures

00:38:24.160 --> 00:38:27.589
that used to work 30 years
ago won't work today, simply

00:38:27.589 --> 00:38:28.880
because the world is different.

00:38:28.880 --> 00:38:30.880
And I don't think
it's Mr. Bush that's

00:38:30.880 --> 00:38:34.885
going to solve this problem.

00:38:34.885 --> 00:38:37.819
AUDIENCE: It's a
related question.

00:38:37.819 --> 00:38:42.290
Does the report have any
specific recommendations

00:38:42.290 --> 00:38:46.560
in respect of the
use of the mass media

00:38:46.560 --> 00:38:49.750
to increase scientific literacy?

00:38:49.750 --> 00:38:53.440
DERTOUZOS: Does the book have
any specific recommendation

00:38:53.440 --> 00:38:55.990
about how the mass
media could help

00:38:55.990 --> 00:38:58.500
increase scientific interaction,
is that what you're asking?

00:38:58.500 --> 00:38:59.410
THUROW: Scientific literacy.

00:38:59.410 --> 00:39:00.370
DERTOUZOS: Scientific literacy.

00:39:00.370 --> 00:39:01.300
Technical literacy.

00:39:01.300 --> 00:39:01.799
I see.

00:39:06.340 --> 00:39:07.150
Yes.

00:39:07.150 --> 00:39:08.830
[CHUCKLING]

00:39:08.830 --> 00:39:13.080
It's a long book, so we'd have
to do a little bit of scanning.

00:39:13.080 --> 00:39:14.290
One of the things--

00:39:14.290 --> 00:39:16.120
look, the problem
is very complex,

00:39:16.120 --> 00:39:18.550
and one cannot look at the
answer to your question

00:39:18.550 --> 00:39:19.470
as the panacea.

00:39:19.470 --> 00:39:22.960
But let me just cite
a couple of things.

00:39:22.960 --> 00:39:25.700
The universities
are the demand side.

00:39:25.700 --> 00:39:27.520
The universities
are the ones who

00:39:27.520 --> 00:39:31.300
take the students that come
from the K through 12 level.

00:39:31.300 --> 00:39:36.460
And there, we say that we
might increase our standards

00:39:36.460 --> 00:39:40.690
and demand a higher preparation
in technical topics.

00:39:40.690 --> 00:39:42.760
But that alone, of
course, is not enough.

00:39:42.760 --> 00:39:44.920
Something must be done
on the supply side,

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:47.500
on the teacher side, got
to motivate the students,

00:39:47.500 --> 00:39:49.420
take into account the
demographic changes,

00:39:49.420 --> 00:39:53.980
and the changes in the ratio
of the population that is going

00:39:53.980 --> 00:39:55.780
to be here in 10, 15 years.

00:39:55.780 --> 00:39:58.810
So a lot more has to be
done on that side as well,

00:39:58.810 --> 00:40:00.110
but there is one possibility.

00:40:00.110 --> 00:40:02.235
The other possibility that
is discussed in the book

00:40:02.235 --> 00:40:05.440
is lengthening the school
year, which in America is now

00:40:05.440 --> 00:40:07.930
one of the shortest among the
school years in the world.

00:40:11.778 --> 00:40:14.183
Yes.

00:40:14.183 --> 00:40:16.588
AUDIENCE: I'd like to go back
to Dean Thurow's comments

00:40:16.588 --> 00:40:19.480
about the media's
responsibility.

00:40:19.480 --> 00:40:22.882
I think the imperatives that
were brought forth today

00:40:22.882 --> 00:40:24.711
are exciting, but
they're also not new.

00:40:24.711 --> 00:40:26.770
It's a story I've
heard before, and I

00:40:26.770 --> 00:40:28.170
think other people have heard.

00:40:28.170 --> 00:40:32.652
The message has been out
for a couple of years

00:40:32.652 --> 00:40:38.130
that America's not number
one anymore in everything.

00:40:38.130 --> 00:40:41.554
The changes are happening, but
they're happening awful slow.

00:40:41.554 --> 00:40:44.110
What can people do
to speed that up?

00:40:44.110 --> 00:40:48.060
And I do think the government
plays a role in that process.

00:40:48.060 --> 00:40:49.560
DERTOUZOS: The
question is, I'd like

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:53.100
to come back to the same issue,
that this is not a new message.

00:40:53.100 --> 00:40:54.840
These are known things.

00:40:54.840 --> 00:40:56.740
And what, really,
should be done?

00:40:56.740 --> 00:40:58.680
What should industry,
government do

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:00.930
to really get things going,
because they're so slow.

00:41:00.930 --> 00:41:03.096
I would like to ask the
panel to consider the answer

00:41:03.096 --> 00:41:06.021
to your question, while I give
you an answer to the statement

00:41:06.021 --> 00:41:08.520
that you made, the assertion
you made, that this is not new.

00:41:11.460 --> 00:41:15.270
As I said, none of the
weaknesses you heard

00:41:15.270 --> 00:41:18.750
is new, because there have
been hundreds of weaknesses

00:41:18.750 --> 00:41:21.090
paraded in the press.

00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:24.270
But that is very much
like a person suffering

00:41:24.270 --> 00:41:27.200
from a large number
of diseases--

00:41:27.200 --> 00:41:30.380
and so he's told, because
everybody who comes by

00:41:30.380 --> 00:41:32.240
sees him, sees that
he's sick, and he

00:41:32.240 --> 00:41:33.560
says, you've got disease x.

00:41:33.560 --> 00:41:35.540
The other person says
you got disease y.

00:41:35.540 --> 00:41:38.000
By the time they're
done, he hears

00:41:38.000 --> 00:41:40.430
that he suffers from
a hundred diseases.

00:41:40.430 --> 00:41:42.530
And then a doctor
comes in and measures

00:41:42.530 --> 00:41:45.650
the temperature, and the
blood, and all the good things,

00:41:45.650 --> 00:41:48.136
and says you have got
only three diseases.

00:41:48.136 --> 00:41:49.760
And then the patient
says, oh, go away.

00:41:49.760 --> 00:41:51.500
I've heard about these diseases.

00:41:51.500 --> 00:41:53.980
These other people
told me about it.

00:41:53.980 --> 00:41:57.050
Now, I want to really
contest the statement

00:41:57.050 --> 00:41:59.270
that these results are not new.

00:41:59.270 --> 00:42:02.960
The results are very new,
because they tell you

00:42:02.960 --> 00:42:05.270
which combination of those
things you've been hearing

00:42:05.270 --> 00:42:08.750
forever matter as
you study the problem

00:42:08.750 --> 00:42:13.190
from the fact from the bottom
up so I think it is new.

00:42:13.190 --> 00:42:16.600
And I think, before that, at
least we at the commission

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:20.570
faced this myriad of
recommendations and questions

00:42:20.570 --> 00:42:22.220
and diagnosis, and
you really couldn't

00:42:22.220 --> 00:42:23.590
find a way through there.

00:42:23.590 --> 00:42:25.340
Now, with that as a
basis, perhaps I can--

00:42:25.340 --> 00:42:27.650
SOLOW: May I say two
things, Mr. Chairman?

00:42:27.650 --> 00:42:29.300
DERTOUZOS: Yes.

00:42:29.300 --> 00:42:32.060
SOLOW: First off, I'm
going to reveal my age

00:42:32.060 --> 00:42:35.450
and tell you that
I remember when

00:42:35.450 --> 00:42:39.820
Rita Hayworth was everybody's
idea of beautiful movie star.

00:42:39.820 --> 00:42:43.890
And, in my youth, there
was a joke that we told--

00:42:43.890 --> 00:42:47.210
a girl says to her boyfriend,
what does Rita Hayworth

00:42:47.210 --> 00:42:49.100
have that I don't have?

00:42:49.100 --> 00:42:51.320
And the boyfriend thinks
for a minute and says,

00:42:51.320 --> 00:42:53.090
nothing, but she
groups it better.

00:42:55.790 --> 00:43:01.040
And our hope is that we
have grouped it better.

00:43:01.040 --> 00:43:06.260
But now let me come to
the rest of the question.

00:43:06.260 --> 00:43:08.330
Somebody asked earlier,
and Les answered

00:43:08.330 --> 00:43:11.570
that question, particularly,
what can the government do

00:43:11.570 --> 00:43:14.600
to inspire this sort of change.

00:43:14.600 --> 00:43:17.000
And I think that Lester
gave the right answer.

00:43:17.000 --> 00:43:19.100
We are not anticipating
inspiration

00:43:19.100 --> 00:43:25.040
from the government
in this respect.

00:43:25.040 --> 00:43:28.310
Most American corporations
understand the problem

00:43:28.310 --> 00:43:29.090
that they face.

00:43:29.090 --> 00:43:32.990
And they need not so much
inspiration as a little bit

00:43:32.990 --> 00:43:36.440
of guidance and some energy.

00:43:36.440 --> 00:43:38.570
I don't think that
Lester meant to suggest

00:43:38.570 --> 00:43:40.550
that there is nothing
that public policy can

00:43:40.550 --> 00:43:44.810
do to improve, or speed up.

00:43:44.810 --> 00:43:47.270
Or facilitate this process.

00:43:47.270 --> 00:43:51.020
There's lots of public policy
out there that's needed.

00:43:51.020 --> 00:43:54.470
The report does not
spell out proposed pieces

00:43:54.470 --> 00:43:57.740
of legislation or regulation
or anything of the sort.

00:43:57.740 --> 00:44:01.340
We didn't think that
that was the right task

00:44:01.340 --> 00:44:05.000
for a group of MIT faculty.

00:44:05.000 --> 00:44:10.750
But one can easily
infer from what

00:44:10.750 --> 00:44:14.670
we have written that policies
that stimulate saving

00:44:14.670 --> 00:44:18.140
and investment in the American
economy that make it easier

00:44:18.140 --> 00:44:21.830
for corporations to
take the long view,

00:44:21.830 --> 00:44:29.390
to commit funds for
risky and long-term

00:44:29.390 --> 00:44:32.780
technological development
projects-- any such policies,

00:44:32.780 --> 00:44:37.670
and we all know what they are,
actually, would be useful.

00:44:37.670 --> 00:44:41.270
The federal government
in this country

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:47.570
has long played a role in
supporting and providing

00:44:47.570 --> 00:44:50.840
incentives for research
and development activity,

00:44:50.840 --> 00:44:53.720
both in universities,
in nonprofit research

00:44:53.720 --> 00:44:56.090
organizations, and
in corporations.

00:44:56.090 --> 00:44:57.780
It ought to be doing more.

00:44:57.780 --> 00:44:59.960
And not only ought
it to be doing more,

00:44:59.960 --> 00:45:02.270
but I think it's a fair
inference, from what we've

00:45:02.270 --> 00:45:06.430
said, that we would like
to see the government

00:45:06.430 --> 00:45:10.280
support of science
and technology

00:45:10.280 --> 00:45:14.960
include support
focused at that part

00:45:14.960 --> 00:45:18.380
of technological development
that lies somewhat closer

00:45:18.380 --> 00:45:22.820
to the commercial production
end, and not simply basic

00:45:22.820 --> 00:45:27.980
research, which is where the
government has specialized up

00:45:27.980 --> 00:45:28.880
until now.

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:33.110
So I you ought not to think
of in this group as believing

00:45:33.110 --> 00:45:36.750
that no public policy could
be useful, or even necessary,

00:45:36.750 --> 00:45:37.250
for this.

00:45:37.250 --> 00:45:38.259
There's lots.

00:45:38.259 --> 00:45:39.800
THUROW: Let me make
an addendum, too.

00:45:39.800 --> 00:45:43.310
I think that I was probably
a little unfair in my earlier

00:45:43.310 --> 00:45:45.590
answer, because I think we
also need some leadership

00:45:45.590 --> 00:45:46.590
from Washington.

00:45:46.590 --> 00:45:48.547
And let me give you a
couple of illustrations.

00:45:48.547 --> 00:45:50.630
Remember back during the
Vice Presidential debates

00:45:50.630 --> 00:45:53.930
in this presidential campaign,
that Vice President Quayle

00:45:53.930 --> 00:45:56.510
basically implied that,
if people in Indiana

00:45:56.510 --> 00:46:00.352
grew Belgian endive that
was somehow unpatriotic.

00:46:00.352 --> 00:46:02.810
Because somebody had claimed
that you would make more money

00:46:02.810 --> 00:46:05.685
growing Belgian endive than
you would American corn.

00:46:05.685 --> 00:46:07.810
Well, you can think of
Belgian endive, if you like,

00:46:07.810 --> 00:46:10.330
as new technology found abroad--

00:46:10.330 --> 00:46:12.990
a product which you can
sell for a high price.

00:46:12.990 --> 00:46:15.580
And if you can grow
Belgian endive in Indiana

00:46:15.580 --> 00:46:17.750
and sell it for a lot more
money per acre than corn,

00:46:17.750 --> 00:46:19.640
then you ought to be
growing Belgian endive.

00:46:19.640 --> 00:46:21.598
But if you're so myopic
you say, the only thing

00:46:21.598 --> 00:46:24.170
that Americans can do
is grow corn, of course

00:46:24.170 --> 00:46:25.760
you're, then, in trouble.

00:46:25.760 --> 00:46:29.520
And I think what we need is
some leadership at the top that

00:46:29.520 --> 00:46:31.030
understands that
we have a problem.

00:46:31.030 --> 00:46:33.570
Now, my impression is that I
think our leaders do understand

00:46:33.570 --> 00:46:34.230
we have a problem.

00:46:34.230 --> 00:46:35.260
But they have
convinced themselves

00:46:35.260 --> 00:46:36.635
that in order to
get elected, you

00:46:36.635 --> 00:46:39.100
have to pretend that you
don't have a problem.

00:46:39.100 --> 00:46:41.850
And I think what that does is
confuse the public horribly,

00:46:41.850 --> 00:46:44.130
because you read about all
the problems in the press.

00:46:44.130 --> 00:46:45.838
And then, during our
political campaigns,

00:46:45.838 --> 00:46:48.187
our leaders all deny that
there are any problems.

00:46:48.187 --> 00:46:49.770
And then, if you're
an average person,

00:46:49.770 --> 00:46:51.400
I think you end up
just conflicted.

00:46:51.400 --> 00:46:52.890
You say, well, maybe
there are some problems,

00:46:52.890 --> 00:46:54.900
but somehow these guys
are running the countries,

00:46:54.900 --> 00:46:56.040
and they don't think
there are any problems,

00:46:56.040 --> 00:46:57.520
or at least that's
what they say.

00:46:57.520 --> 00:47:00.060
And so I think one of the things
we have to do at the government

00:47:00.060 --> 00:47:01.590
level is put it out
on the table what

00:47:01.590 --> 00:47:03.300
we believe about these
problems, as opposed

00:47:03.300 --> 00:47:04.770
to saying that the
only thing that

00:47:04.770 --> 00:47:07.230
sells to the American public
in an election campaign

00:47:07.230 --> 00:47:10.710
is gross rank optimism.

00:47:10.710 --> 00:47:13.590
DERTOUZOS: The book
has nine points

00:47:13.590 --> 00:47:15.599
which are recommendations
to government.

00:47:15.599 --> 00:47:17.640
And they're the translation
of these imperatives.

00:47:17.640 --> 00:47:18.326
Yes.

00:47:18.326 --> 00:47:23.163
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] Why do
you think that the people--

00:47:23.163 --> 00:47:28.165
the recipients of the message
is the broad public, and not

00:47:28.165 --> 00:47:31.900
the people who are
responsible for the changes

00:47:31.900 --> 00:47:34.280
in the corporations,
for the directors.

00:47:34.280 --> 00:47:37.590
Wouldn't it be more effective
to bring this message directly

00:47:37.590 --> 00:47:40.360
to these responsible people?

00:47:40.360 --> 00:47:42.335
DERTOUZOS: The
question is, who ought

00:47:42.335 --> 00:47:45.290
to be the recipient of
our message, the public,

00:47:45.290 --> 00:47:46.960
or the more responsible people?

00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:49.570
I presume you mean corporate
executives, government

00:47:49.570 --> 00:47:50.110
officials.

00:47:50.110 --> 00:47:51.400
THUROW: I think the answer,
in the United States,

00:47:51.400 --> 00:47:52.210
has to be both.

00:47:52.210 --> 00:47:55.600
Because we don't quite have
an establishment in the sense

00:47:55.600 --> 00:47:57.970
that some other countries
have an establishment that

00:47:57.970 --> 00:48:01.810
came kind of, in some sense,
ram things down people's throat.

00:48:01.810 --> 00:48:04.000
And so, when you say the
American establishment

00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:05.680
or the Japanese
establishment, I think

00:48:05.680 --> 00:48:07.846
you're talking about very
different groups of people

00:48:07.846 --> 00:48:08.992
and what they can do.

00:48:08.992 --> 00:48:11.200
And since we don't have an
establishment in the sense

00:48:11.200 --> 00:48:13.120
that Japan has an
establishment, I

00:48:13.120 --> 00:48:14.830
think a much broader
group of people

00:48:14.830 --> 00:48:17.920
have to be convinced that change
is necessary in the United

00:48:17.920 --> 00:48:20.440
States than perhaps
have to be convinced

00:48:20.440 --> 00:48:21.440
in some other countries.

00:48:21.440 --> 00:48:23.170
And of course, the
other thing that

00:48:23.170 --> 00:48:25.628
is true in the United States
that you have to remember that

00:48:25.628 --> 00:48:27.280
is not true, for
example, in Europe--

00:48:27.280 --> 00:48:29.590
most Americans never
leave the United States.

00:48:29.590 --> 00:48:31.754
Nine out of 10 don't
leave the United States.

00:48:31.754 --> 00:48:34.170
And so they have no experience
with the rest of the world,

00:48:34.170 --> 00:48:35.920
and don't see these
things firsthand.

00:48:35.920 --> 00:48:38.590
Well, if you're a European, just
when you go on your vacation,

00:48:38.590 --> 00:48:39.880
you're going to see
some other countries,

00:48:39.880 --> 00:48:42.490
be someplace where somebody
speaks a different language,

00:48:42.490 --> 00:48:44.920
where the refrigerators
don't look exactly the same,

00:48:44.920 --> 00:48:46.310
those kind of things.

00:48:46.310 --> 00:48:48.910
And so I think this problem
of kind of educating Americans

00:48:48.910 --> 00:48:50.618
as to what the rest
of the world is like,

00:48:50.618 --> 00:48:53.020
and how it forces them to
adjust their procedures

00:48:53.020 --> 00:48:55.292
is much harder, simply
because we are so large.

00:48:55.292 --> 00:48:57.250
And, you know, there are
five million Americans

00:48:57.250 --> 00:48:59.000
who go to Europe every
summer, but they're

00:48:59.000 --> 00:49:00.495
the same five
million every year.

00:49:00.495 --> 00:49:02.620
DERTOUZOS: We have a couple
of more questions here.

00:49:02.620 --> 00:49:04.180
You were first, yes.

00:49:04.180 --> 00:49:05.680
AUDIENCE: I think
that some of the--

00:49:05.680 --> 00:49:06.370
DERTOUZOS: Could you
identify yourself?

00:49:06.370 --> 00:49:07.831
AUDIENCE: My name
is [INAUDIBLE]..

00:49:07.831 --> 00:49:10.266
I am a Knight Fellow.

00:49:10.266 --> 00:49:12.570
I think that some of those
[INAUDIBLE] production

00:49:12.570 --> 00:49:15.050
or head of finance
are really difficult,

00:49:15.050 --> 00:49:18.890
and change the
implication of a system.

00:49:18.890 --> 00:49:22.490
So I think, after this book
and have those suggestions,

00:49:22.490 --> 00:49:24.284
how do you think you
could implement them?

00:49:24.284 --> 00:49:26.618
Like [INAUDIBLE]
with leading persons

00:49:26.618 --> 00:49:30.540
from the educational
institutions

00:49:30.540 --> 00:49:34.520
and industries and government.

00:49:34.520 --> 00:49:41.710
Like, to-- to form a leading
group like to put [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:41.710 --> 00:49:45.690
And also, here, at
MIT, you are already

00:49:45.690 --> 00:49:49.167
thinking about doing
something like that.

00:49:49.167 --> 00:49:50.750
DERTOUZOS: OK, the
question is, what's

00:49:50.750 --> 00:49:52.950
the best way to
implement-- or how

00:49:52.950 --> 00:49:57.050
are we going to implement
--what we preach in this book.

00:49:57.050 --> 00:49:58.040
Professor Lester.

00:49:58.040 --> 00:50:04.290
LESTER: Well, let me address
the MIT part of the question.

00:50:04.290 --> 00:50:06.710
One of the things that
the report points out,

00:50:06.710 --> 00:50:10.760
and that Mike
mentioned, is that we

00:50:10.760 --> 00:50:14.060
have observed, in a number of
the industries we looked at,

00:50:14.060 --> 00:50:17.090
weaknesses that we call
downstream technical

00:50:17.090 --> 00:50:19.100
weaknesses-- weaknesses
in product design,

00:50:19.100 --> 00:50:21.690
process design, manufacturing.

00:50:21.690 --> 00:50:23.000
And, to some extent--

00:50:23.000 --> 00:50:26.810
although not entirely
--that set of weaknesses

00:50:26.810 --> 00:50:33.470
can be traced to developments--
an evolution in the way we

00:50:33.470 --> 00:50:36.830
think, or have thought about,
engineering education at MIT

00:50:36.830 --> 00:50:40.400
and at the other leading
research universities

00:50:40.400 --> 00:50:43.490
and technical universities.

00:50:43.490 --> 00:50:46.910
Within the last couple of
years, as you may have heard,

00:50:46.910 --> 00:50:49.190
we have made a
number of attempts

00:50:49.190 --> 00:50:53.360
to rethink that to some degree.

00:50:53.360 --> 00:50:58.610
And one of the things we've done
is introduce a master's program

00:50:58.610 --> 00:51:00.740
joined with Sloan
and Engineering,

00:51:00.740 --> 00:51:07.910
directed towards increasing
the production, if you like,

00:51:07.910 --> 00:51:11.780
of engineering and
management-oriented people who

00:51:11.780 --> 00:51:15.830
know about and will
practice manufacturing.

00:51:15.830 --> 00:51:18.440
That's one of the things
that we've tried to do.

00:51:18.440 --> 00:51:26.450
But in fact, our report
calls for a broader approach

00:51:26.450 --> 00:51:29.630
to some of these
problems within MIT,

00:51:29.630 --> 00:51:32.900
in which we would hope that,
right across the Institute,

00:51:32.900 --> 00:51:35.720
more attention would
be given, for example,

00:51:35.720 --> 00:51:40.660
to real manufacturing
production problems--

00:51:40.660 --> 00:51:43.850
also, more attention, as
Les was talking about,

00:51:43.850 --> 00:51:47.195
to the international
aspects of this problem.

00:51:47.195 --> 00:51:49.070
So we think that there
are a number of things

00:51:49.070 --> 00:51:51.500
that we can do at MIT--

00:51:51.500 --> 00:51:53.300
and maybe other
research universities

00:51:53.300 --> 00:51:55.850
should also be doing
--to make us, at least,

00:51:55.850 --> 00:51:57.965
more responsive to
some of these problems.

00:51:57.965 --> 00:51:59.840
DERTOUZOS: This, of
course, is what we should

00:51:59.840 --> 00:52:01.580
do at home, which comes first.

00:52:01.580 --> 00:52:04.310
And we spell out quite a bit of
what MIT and other universities

00:52:04.310 --> 00:52:05.390
should do.

00:52:05.390 --> 00:52:07.250
Insofar as industry
is concerned,

00:52:07.250 --> 00:52:09.710
besides putting
out our book, we're

00:52:09.710 --> 00:52:11.870
having a lot of meetings
with the commission

00:52:11.870 --> 00:52:15.440
and the CEOs of companies,
groups, the alumni of MIT.

00:52:15.440 --> 00:52:17.240
So we're spreading
the word that way.

00:52:17.240 --> 00:52:19.290
And, insofar as
government is concerned,

00:52:19.290 --> 00:52:22.186
we are having a briefing at the
executive branch next Monday,

00:52:22.186 --> 00:52:24.560
and we're having a briefing
in Congress in the afternoon,

00:52:24.560 --> 00:52:28.170
and we're testifying before
Senator Kennedy's committee

00:52:28.170 --> 00:52:29.840
on Wednesday.

00:52:29.840 --> 00:52:32.610
I think we're doing the best we
can, which a university can do,

00:52:32.610 --> 00:52:35.660
which is disseminate its
results through the media

00:52:35.660 --> 00:52:36.905
and through the written word.

00:52:36.905 --> 00:52:37.759
Yes, ma'am.

00:52:37.759 --> 00:52:40.054
AUDIENCE: Do you already have
reactions, from industry,

00:52:40.054 --> 00:52:42.350
to your report?

00:52:42.350 --> 00:52:43.310
DERTOUZOS: Yes, we do.

00:52:43.310 --> 00:52:45.110
We have several reactions.

00:52:45.110 --> 00:52:46.760
And perhaps they
are our friends,

00:52:46.760 --> 00:52:48.380
but they're running
fairly positive.

00:52:48.380 --> 00:52:51.050
In fact, the back of the book
has quite a few endorsements

00:52:51.050 --> 00:52:52.800
from industry.

00:52:52.800 --> 00:52:56.520
We better wait till this is
out and see the other comments,

00:52:56.520 --> 00:52:57.020
as well.

00:52:57.020 --> 00:52:57.815
Yes, sir.

00:52:57.815 --> 00:52:58.963
AUDIENCE: I'd like a
little more information

00:52:58.963 --> 00:53:00.129
about some of your evidence.

00:53:00.129 --> 00:53:02.482
Are there some
specific companies,

00:53:02.482 --> 00:53:04.150
where you can point
to companies that

00:53:04.150 --> 00:53:06.870
are in a similar situation as
each other, but you can say,

00:53:06.870 --> 00:53:08.777
this one looks at
profits 10 years

00:53:08.777 --> 00:53:11.110
from now instead of next
quarter, and does a better job.

00:53:11.110 --> 00:53:14.750
This one treats workers as
an asset instead of a cost,

00:53:14.750 --> 00:53:15.990
and has succeeded better.

00:53:15.990 --> 00:53:16.989
What are some specific--

00:53:16.989 --> 00:53:20.670
DERTOUZOS: The question is to
give some specific examples

00:53:20.670 --> 00:53:21.980
of the studies we have done.

00:53:21.980 --> 00:53:23.750
I'd like to call in
Professor Lester.

00:53:23.750 --> 00:53:27.080
LESTER: Well, I think one of the
most striking comparisons that

00:53:27.080 --> 00:53:33.230
emerged from our work came
out of the steel industry,

00:53:33.230 --> 00:53:37.770
where we looked at both
integrated steel companies--

00:53:37.770 --> 00:53:40.940
large companies that
basically do everything

00:53:40.940 --> 00:53:44.780
--but also, we looked
at and spent some time

00:53:44.780 --> 00:53:48.020
visiting some of the mini
mill companies, which

00:53:48.020 --> 00:53:50.750
have been quite successful,
over the last several years,

00:53:50.750 --> 00:53:53.540
in grabbing market share
from the larger companies.

00:53:53.540 --> 00:53:56.840
And we did see pretty
striking differences

00:53:56.840 --> 00:53:59.220
in many of these dimensions.

00:53:59.220 --> 00:54:02.270
One dimension in
particular that I point to

00:54:02.270 --> 00:54:08.120
would be the way these
companies handled human resource

00:54:08.120 --> 00:54:09.230
questions.

00:54:09.230 --> 00:54:13.220
One of the most striking pieces
of evidence in this respect

00:54:13.220 --> 00:54:17.540
concerned Chaparral Steel,
which is a small mini mill

00:54:17.540 --> 00:54:20.750
company located in Texas.

00:54:20.750 --> 00:54:26.750
In order to learn
about what was going

00:54:26.750 --> 00:54:28.730
on around the world in
their particular field,

00:54:28.730 --> 00:54:33.470
and in order to import the best
practices, both organization

00:54:33.470 --> 00:54:39.060
and technical, that company
sent groups of people--

00:54:39.060 --> 00:54:43.580
including production workers and
maintenance workers, as well as

00:54:43.580 --> 00:54:46.190
white collar people
within the company

00:54:46.190 --> 00:54:48.470
--sent them around the world,
gave them travel funds,

00:54:48.470 --> 00:54:51.290
said, you go out and
learn what you can

00:54:51.290 --> 00:54:53.810
about what our best
competitors are doing,

00:54:53.810 --> 00:54:56.690
and come back and
implement them.

00:54:56.690 --> 00:55:00.080
That company also, for example,
sent its production workers

00:55:00.080 --> 00:55:03.120
out-- people that
worked on the line,

00:55:03.120 --> 00:55:07.340
so to speak --to talk
to their customers,

00:55:07.340 --> 00:55:10.640
to find out what those customers
thought of their product,

00:55:10.640 --> 00:55:13.000
and what they can
do to improve it.

00:55:13.000 --> 00:55:15.050
What one saw in this
particular company--

00:55:15.050 --> 00:55:17.840
and I think this is an
example that that would apply

00:55:17.840 --> 00:55:20.780
in a number of other
industries --what one saw

00:55:20.780 --> 00:55:24.350
was a kind of a breaking down
of some traditional functional

00:55:24.350 --> 00:55:28.760
areas, functional
activities within a company,

00:55:28.760 --> 00:55:31.850
making the company
more open, so to speak,

00:55:31.850 --> 00:55:35.280
to the flow of
information from outside.

00:55:35.280 --> 00:55:38.060
And this was a fairly
strong contrast

00:55:38.060 --> 00:55:41.510
from what we observed in some
of the older, larger steel

00:55:41.510 --> 00:55:42.830
companies.

00:55:42.830 --> 00:55:45.260
DERTOUZOS: Let me just
give you an extension

00:55:45.260 --> 00:55:46.740
so you can see more examples.

00:55:46.740 --> 00:55:49.070
The second half of the
book Made in America

00:55:49.070 --> 00:55:51.470
is full of various of
these examples in the eight

00:55:51.470 --> 00:55:52.430
industries.

00:55:52.430 --> 00:55:55.550
And three weeks from now, we
are publishing through the MIT

00:55:55.550 --> 00:55:57.080
Press the nine volumes--

00:55:57.080 --> 00:55:58.788
there are actually
two volumes, but there

00:55:58.788 --> 00:56:01.580
are nine sets of working
papers of the commission, which

00:56:01.580 --> 00:56:03.920
include all the detailed
examples --so that you will

00:56:03.920 --> 00:56:06.200
have an ample
opportunity to get more

00:56:06.200 --> 00:56:08.659
of the bottom-up material that
drove us to our conclusions.

00:56:08.659 --> 00:56:11.241
THUROW: Now, see, part of this
is also just kind of a mindset.

00:56:11.241 --> 00:56:13.880
Now, this is not a comparison
so much among American firms,

00:56:13.880 --> 00:56:17.030
but if you look at the textiles
section of this report,

00:56:17.030 --> 00:56:19.280
the conventional wisdom,
kind of, of the United States

00:56:19.280 --> 00:56:21.170
is, textiles is a
low-wage industry,

00:56:21.170 --> 00:56:22.550
and you can't
compete in textiles

00:56:22.550 --> 00:56:25.862
if you're not mainland
China and paying $2 a day.

00:56:25.862 --> 00:56:27.320
The problem with
that conception is

00:56:27.320 --> 00:56:30.020
that there is a very successful
high-wage textile industry

00:56:30.020 --> 00:56:32.370
in a country like Germany.

00:56:32.370 --> 00:56:34.730
And it may be true,
in textiles, you

00:56:34.730 --> 00:56:37.070
can't do everything
and pay high wages.

00:56:37.070 --> 00:56:39.390
But if you very carefully
put it together, in fact,

00:56:39.390 --> 00:56:42.170
there's a very viable,
reasonably-large textile

00:56:42.170 --> 00:56:44.630
industry that can
be built that is

00:56:44.630 --> 00:56:46.970
survivable in a high-wage
industrial country,

00:56:46.970 --> 00:56:48.592
as the Germans demonstrate.

00:56:48.592 --> 00:56:51.050
And so the question then is,
can you put that kind of thing

00:56:51.050 --> 00:56:52.520
together in the United States.

00:56:52.520 --> 00:56:55.670
And there doesn't seem to be any
intrinsic reason, because you

00:56:55.670 --> 00:56:57.470
can find individual
firms that are starting

00:56:57.470 --> 00:56:58.564
to move in that direction.

00:56:58.564 --> 00:57:00.980
But the problem is, you have
to do it as a whole industry.

00:57:00.980 --> 00:57:02.930
You can't do it on an
individual firm basis.

00:57:02.930 --> 00:57:04.388
Because if you've
got one firm that

00:57:04.388 --> 00:57:07.470
is very good at making cloth,
that doesn't save the industry.

00:57:07.470 --> 00:57:10.280
You've also got to have
other people to put together

00:57:10.280 --> 00:57:14.580
the garment part of it, perhaps
the chemical part of it,

00:57:14.580 --> 00:57:15.500
and so forth.

00:57:15.500 --> 00:57:19.102
And so, you know, it is possible
to do some things that were--

00:57:19.102 --> 00:57:21.560
and if you have these blinders
that say, that's impossible,

00:57:21.560 --> 00:57:23.540
you just abandoned
an industry that

00:57:23.540 --> 00:57:25.010
shouldn't be fully abandoned.

00:57:25.010 --> 00:57:26.690
DERTOUZOS: We have just
a couple of minutes left.

00:57:26.690 --> 00:57:28.010
So let's see, you
had a question.

00:57:28.010 --> 00:57:28.970
AUDIENCE: I just want to follow.

00:57:28.970 --> 00:57:31.070
To the extent that some
of these differences

00:57:31.070 --> 00:57:34.810
are obvious in companies
that exist or are observable,

00:57:34.810 --> 00:57:37.730
and your comment that other
companies see these as

00:57:37.730 --> 00:57:39.210
successful, but can't switch.

00:57:39.210 --> 00:57:40.190
It's a wrench in it.

00:57:40.190 --> 00:57:44.080
They can't survive as
a company, necessarily,

00:57:44.080 --> 00:57:48.354
if US Steel tries
to become Chaparral.

00:57:48.354 --> 00:57:50.020
Do some of these
changes mean that there

00:57:50.020 --> 00:57:51.610
has to be a different
set of companies,

00:57:51.610 --> 00:57:52.990
that we're going to have
new companies that are

00:57:52.990 --> 00:57:54.520
built on these new principles?

00:57:54.520 --> 00:57:56.622
Or can existing companies
make these changes?

00:57:56.622 --> 00:57:59.080
DERTOUZOS: The question is, of
course, since these changes,

00:57:59.080 --> 00:58:02.680
the imperatives, are so
many and so wrenching,

00:58:02.680 --> 00:58:04.180
what does this mean?

00:58:04.180 --> 00:58:06.400
How are we going to be
able to diffuse them

00:58:06.400 --> 00:58:09.496
in American industry?

00:58:09.496 --> 00:58:11.430
THUROW: Well, I suspect
the truthful answer

00:58:11.430 --> 00:58:14.350
is there are going to
be some companies that

00:58:14.350 --> 00:58:17.440
can't change, and will
effectively go out of business.

00:58:17.440 --> 00:58:19.390
But you can find
examples of big companies

00:58:19.390 --> 00:58:21.860
that are trying to make
these kind of changes,

00:58:21.860 --> 00:58:25.150
and I think some
reasonably successfully.

00:58:25.150 --> 00:58:27.790
I suppose one of the examples
that would be common today, you

00:58:27.790 --> 00:58:29.041
know, if you think about Ford.

00:58:29.041 --> 00:58:30.915
SOLOW: Well, let me give
you an example which

00:58:30.915 --> 00:58:32.940
hasn't played itself out
yet, but is, after all,

00:58:32.940 --> 00:58:34.840
on the larger scale.

00:58:34.840 --> 00:58:36.700
Roughly with this
sort of thing in mind,

00:58:36.700 --> 00:58:38.180
although not
precisely, of course,

00:58:38.180 --> 00:58:40.150
the details that
are in this book.

00:58:40.150 --> 00:58:42.070
When the General
Motors Corporation

00:58:42.070 --> 00:58:46.000
decided to build a car that
they wanted to call the Saturn,

00:58:46.000 --> 00:58:51.640
they decided to build it
almost as an entirely separate

00:58:51.640 --> 00:58:52.810
company--

00:58:52.810 --> 00:58:57.940
different place, a sort of
self-contained management,

00:58:57.940 --> 00:59:01.495
simply to be almost independent
of the rest of the corporation.

00:59:01.495 --> 00:59:06.400
So if even, sort of, the
arch-typical big corporation

00:59:06.400 --> 00:59:09.280
like GM can do that, then I
suppose other corporations

00:59:09.280 --> 00:59:10.220
can do the same.

00:59:10.220 --> 00:59:12.070
THUROW: To the famous
German luxury cars--

00:59:12.070 --> 00:59:14.650
the Mercedes-Benz and the BMWs.

00:59:14.650 --> 00:59:18.250
And so, you know, it is possible
to recapture these markets.

00:59:18.250 --> 00:59:20.980
And you can see examples,
if you look at luxury cars,

00:59:20.980 --> 00:59:23.200
where the sales of foreign
luxury cars are down,

00:59:23.200 --> 00:59:24.750
you can turn those
things around.

00:59:24.750 --> 00:59:26.000
DERTOUZOS: Two more questions.

00:59:26.000 --> 00:59:27.629
Yes.

00:59:27.629 --> 00:59:31.010
AUDIENCE: Some economic
practices and beliefs

00:59:31.010 --> 00:59:34.906
have very deep roots in
the American society,

00:59:34.906 --> 00:59:41.034
to the point that they look
more to foreigners like dogma.

00:59:41.034 --> 00:59:44.643
For example, integration
of the state and economy.

00:59:44.643 --> 00:59:47.370
And the evidence
in other countries

00:59:47.370 --> 00:59:49.722
show that there is
a lot of integration

00:59:49.722 --> 00:59:52.310
in every [INAUDIBLE].

00:59:52.310 --> 00:59:55.960
How could you consolidate
that economic culture

00:59:55.960 --> 01:00:00.010
with the [INAUDIBLE] evolution
of the American society,

01:00:00.010 --> 01:00:05.450
and [INAUDIBLE] with the
battles in the Congress.

01:00:05.450 --> 01:00:09.160
Could you describe a shopping
list for the Congress?

01:00:09.160 --> 01:00:12.640
DERTOUZOS: I think the question
has to do with the fact

01:00:12.640 --> 01:00:16.390
that there seemed to be some
deeply rooted American--

01:00:16.390 --> 01:00:19.130
some cultural aspects
in American society,

01:00:19.130 --> 01:00:22.600
and how do we reconcile
this with what we are doing,

01:00:22.600 --> 01:00:26.042
and what we advocate that
we should be doing, right.

01:00:26.042 --> 01:00:29.346
AUDIENCE: But I am referring
to the economic aspect--

01:00:29.346 --> 01:00:31.900
to the economic
theory and beliefs.

01:00:34.670 --> 01:00:36.670
DERTOUZOS: Oh, that there
is a different mindset

01:00:36.670 --> 01:00:38.710
in this country about
economic beliefs?

01:00:38.710 --> 01:00:39.460
Is that what you--

01:00:39.460 --> 01:00:40.960
AUDIENCE: Yes, and
the participation

01:00:40.960 --> 01:00:42.629
of the state in the--

01:00:42.629 --> 01:00:44.920
DERTOUZOS: The involvement
of the state in the economy.

01:00:44.920 --> 01:00:45.420
I see.

01:00:45.420 --> 01:00:46.319
I see.

01:00:46.319 --> 01:00:47.110
Well, I don't know.

01:00:47.110 --> 01:00:49.420
One of our distinguished
economists?

01:00:49.420 --> 01:00:50.710
Professor Lester.

01:00:50.710 --> 01:00:53.560
THUROW: I think your statement
is basically right that we like

01:00:53.560 --> 01:00:55.990
to pretend that we're
pragmatists, but in some ways

01:00:55.990 --> 01:00:57.355
we are very dogmatic.

01:00:57.355 --> 01:00:59.230
And when it comes to
the idea that government

01:00:59.230 --> 01:01:02.800
can't do anything well, I think
we are extremely dogmatic.

01:01:02.800 --> 01:01:04.462
And I think that's
a bit of dogmatism

01:01:04.462 --> 01:01:05.920
that we're going
to have to modify.

01:01:05.920 --> 01:01:08.086
For example, if you look
at research and development

01:01:08.086 --> 01:01:10.690
in every country in the world,
at least half, and usually 2/3

01:01:10.690 --> 01:01:12.780
of the funds, one way or
another, come from government.

01:01:12.780 --> 01:01:14.529
And government plays
a very important role

01:01:14.529 --> 01:01:17.407
because of what economists
know as externalities.

01:01:17.407 --> 01:01:19.990
And we're going to have to face
up to some of those realities.

01:01:19.990 --> 01:01:22.450
Now obviously, the
trick of any society

01:01:22.450 --> 01:01:23.920
being successful
over the long run

01:01:23.920 --> 01:01:26.710
is, can you change
your dogmatism.

01:01:26.710 --> 01:01:28.870
You may be a crusader
at every point in time,

01:01:28.870 --> 01:01:30.580
but you crusade for
different things

01:01:30.580 --> 01:01:31.836
as the decades roll along.

01:01:31.836 --> 01:01:34.210
And I think that's going to
be the test over the next two

01:01:34.210 --> 01:01:37.210
or three years, is, can we
decide which of these dogmas

01:01:37.210 --> 01:01:39.000
are really functional
in the 21st century,

01:01:39.000 --> 01:01:40.708
and which of these
dogmas, in some sense,

01:01:40.708 --> 01:01:43.360
have to be discarded to be
successful in the 21st century.

01:01:43.360 --> 01:01:45.193
SOLOW: Actually, no, I
think that part of it

01:01:45.193 --> 01:01:46.420
is the easiest part of it.

01:01:46.420 --> 01:01:49.090
Government is the sphere
in which the human race has

01:01:49.090 --> 01:01:52.545
developed the greatest
capacity to say one thing

01:01:52.545 --> 01:01:53.170
and do another.

01:01:57.250 --> 01:02:00.310
I think the harder
problem will be

01:02:00.310 --> 01:02:05.020
what you might describe as
the culture of industry,

01:02:05.020 --> 01:02:08.800
the notion in this country,
for instance, that trade unions

01:02:08.800 --> 01:02:11.880
and management
are bitter enemies

01:02:11.880 --> 01:02:13.570
and must always
be bitter enemies,

01:02:13.570 --> 01:02:18.130
and concessions or
cooperation between them

01:02:18.130 --> 01:02:20.710
is a sign to the rank and
file that somebody is being

01:02:20.710 --> 01:02:23.440
betrayed-- that sort of thing.

01:02:23.440 --> 01:02:28.720
The notion that
industrial operations

01:02:28.720 --> 01:02:32.410
have to be simplified, that
the ideal way to produce

01:02:32.410 --> 01:02:34.720
a complicated object
is to break it down

01:02:34.720 --> 01:02:42.990
into the most elementary bits
so that an uneducated person,

01:02:42.990 --> 01:02:45.360
or even a primitive
robot, can do it.

01:02:45.360 --> 01:02:48.180
That's the sort of thing I
think will be harder to change.

01:02:48.180 --> 01:02:52.530
And it will change,
not overnight,

01:02:52.530 --> 01:02:56.820
but in an evolutionary way.

01:02:56.820 --> 01:02:58.950
DERTOUZOS: Professor
Lester wanted to comment.

01:02:58.950 --> 01:03:01.230
LESTER: Well, I think
Bob said pretty much what

01:03:01.230 --> 01:03:03.030
I was going to say.

01:03:03.030 --> 01:03:08.090
Those kinds of changes
don't seem, to me at least,

01:03:08.090 --> 01:03:10.530
to be changes that
you can legislate.

01:03:10.530 --> 01:03:15.641
This is not the kind of thing
the government can pass laws

01:03:15.641 --> 01:03:16.140
about.

01:03:16.140 --> 01:03:18.450
These are attitudinal
changes, sometimes quite

01:03:18.450 --> 01:03:20.790
fundamental changes,
that will have

01:03:20.790 --> 01:03:23.400
to take place over a
long period of time,

01:03:23.400 --> 01:03:26.010
and involve all segments of
society, not just, or even

01:03:26.010 --> 01:03:27.070
primarily, government.

01:03:27.070 --> 01:03:29.070
DERTOUZOS: I know you
have a lot more questions,

01:03:29.070 --> 01:03:30.780
but we said we would
stop right now.

01:03:30.780 --> 01:03:36.000
Let me just close this
briefing by giving you

01:03:36.000 --> 01:03:38.920
a point of view
on this question.

01:03:38.920 --> 01:03:42.300
Let us not forget that
this nation is a little bit

01:03:42.300 --> 01:03:44.310
different than other nations.

01:03:44.310 --> 01:03:46.830
It is not homogenous.

01:03:46.830 --> 01:03:49.200
It has, in it, people
from every possible land.

01:03:49.200 --> 01:03:53.220
And it has proven in the past
that it can adapt to change.

01:03:53.220 --> 01:03:56.180
When it comes to
culture, I personally

01:03:56.180 --> 01:03:59.130
do not believe that the
cultural differences are

01:03:59.130 --> 01:04:01.950
behind what we have seen here.

01:04:01.950 --> 01:04:05.040
You see the NUMMI plant where
American workers are doing just

01:04:05.040 --> 01:04:07.290
as well as the Japanese
workers, in terms

01:04:07.290 --> 01:04:10.390
of the defects of the cars
that come out, and so forth.

01:04:10.390 --> 01:04:13.480
So I think that the
five imperatives

01:04:13.480 --> 01:04:15.952
list what are the things
that have to be done.

01:04:15.952 --> 01:04:17.410
This nation has
enough youthfulness

01:04:17.410 --> 01:04:21.780
in it, enough diversity in its
various ethnic and cultural

01:04:21.780 --> 01:04:23.400
groups to be able
to cope with it,

01:04:23.400 --> 01:04:26.470
and, if we pull together--
government, industry,

01:04:26.470 --> 01:04:27.870
and educational
institutions --we

01:04:27.870 --> 01:04:29.797
believe that it can be done.

01:04:29.797 --> 01:04:30.630
Thank you very much.

01:04:33.330 --> 01:04:34.880
No need.

